**88.9% to 64.8%** of fully diluted shares, a drop of roughly **24 percentage points**. That is the number to anchor on as you negotiate terms.\n\n### Where the dilution comes from\n\nThree things are driving the 24pp drop, in order of impact:\n\n1. **New investor money ($2M in)** \u2014 accounts for about 20pp of the dilution. This is the straightforward trade: new shares issued to new investors at the Series A price.\n2. **Convertible note conversion** \u2014 accounts for about 4pp. Your outstanding note converts into equity at this round, adding shares to the cap table alongside the new money.\n3. **SAFE conversion** \u2014 accounts for about 3pp. The outstanding SAFE also converts at this round, similarly adding shares.\n\nTogether the converting instruments (note + SAFE) cost you roughly 7pp on top of the headline dilution from new money. That is worth understanding before you negotiate: the \"size of the round\" the investors quote will often mean only the new money, but your actual dilution includes the conversions too.\n\nNo option pool top-up was modeled for this round. If you are planning to top up the pool as part of the Series A (which is common \u2014 investors often require it), that would add further dilution on top of the 24pp above. The pool's sizing and whether it is carved out pre-money or post-money can shift founders' ownership by several percentage points; raise that with counsel before you finalize the term sheet.\n\n### One thing to confirm before the math is final\n\nOne of the two instruments was extracted at medium confidence \u2014 there is still one outstanding item to confirm against the source document. That instrument's terms feed directly into the conversion math above, so the 64.8% post-round figure should be treated as directional until the confirmation is resolved. The report flags which field needs checking.\n\n### A timing question to bring to counsel: QSBS\n\nThere is one counsel-review item in the Delaware cross-border domain: